 \subsection{RQ3: Explanatory Conditions}
 \label{sec:rq3_explanatory}
-RQ2 shows that a sparse-stratum ECE rise is visible on some datasets and absent on others. RQ3 asks when that rise is estimable at all. Using only training-fold frequencies, training-only KC covariates, and the event-level SimpleKT ECE already reported in Table~\ref{tab:calibration_learner}, Section~\ref{sec:a5_dataset_dependent} isolates three measurable pre-conditions: (1)~a non-empty low-frequency tail under the split and thresholds actually used; (2)~enough sparse test events for at least a Limited-flag ECE; and (3)~a frequency--difficulty coupling that is not inverted. ASSISTments 2012 meets all three and shows a monotonic ECE increase ($0.113\to 0.158\to 0.225$). Junyi Academy fails (1) and (2) on the learner-based split, so the protocol leaves sparse ECE blank rather than imputing stability. XES3G5M meets (1) and (2) but not (3); SimpleKT ECE is flat, while IRT is inverted and DKT increases. These are diagnostic pre-conditions, not a claim that training frequency causes miscalibration.
+RQ2 shows that a sparse-stratum ECE rise is visible on some datasets and absent on others. RQ3 asks when that rise is estimable at all. Using only training-fold frequencies, training-only KC covariates, and the event-level SimpleKT ECE already reported in Table~\ref{tab:calibration_learner}, Section~\ref{sec:a5_dataset_dependent} isolates three measurable pre-conditions: (1)~a non-empty low-frequency tail under the split and thresholds actually used; (2)~enough sparse test events for at least a Limited-flag ECE; and (3)~a frequency--difficulty coupling that is not inverted. ASSISTments 2012 meets all three and shows a monotonic ECE increase ($0.113\to 0.158\to 0.225$). Junyi Academy fails (1) and (2) on the learner-based split, so the protocol leaves sparse ECE blank rather than imputing stability. XES3G5M meets (1) and (2) but not (3); SimpleKT ECE is flat, while IRT is inverted and DKT increases. These are diagnostic pre-conditions, not a claim that training frequency causes miscalibration. Appendix~\ref{app:a9_sparsify} further tests a controlled reduction of training rows for the same originally dense KCs: a within-KC ECE increase appears in some dataset--model cells (notably SimpleKT on Junyi Academy) and is absent in others (DKT and SimpleKT on ASSISTments 2012). Observable KC characteristics (Section~\ref{sec:a4_confounding}) explain part of the observational heterogeneity (Finding~4); they do not reduce it to a single frequency dose--response.
 
 That answer is still about \emph{observed vulnerability}. Whether sparse-concept diagnostics are \emph{worth running as a high-priority analysis} additionally depends on evaluation support, intended use of probabilities, and temporal cold-start exposure. Section~\ref{sec:a8_when_needed} states that distinction as a conditional-use framework (Tables~\ref{tab:a8_need_framework} and~\ref{tab:a8_dataset_need}). Occupancy reporting remains useful even when the empirical priority of sparse-stratum claims is low.
 
